From 4ca9d9fe32d55a8c738d9ac1dd7af6ec20181608 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 05:03:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09893/11362] [outer-loop] Restore guard-fix hunks a conductor
 checkpoint deleted

A conductor checkpoint commit, 2b5d340c70 "preserve uncommitted work from
interrupted run", committed a stale working tree over 5e74c37b7c twelve minutes
after it landed. It removed 73 lines across two files with zero insertions -- a
pure deletion, which is why restoring from 5e74c37b7c is exact and loses nothing.

What it destroyed:

  scripts/audit_findings_ledger.py       61 lines: EXCLUDED_SOURCES,
                                         discover_milestone_close_audits(),
                                         unclassified_audits()
  scripts/qa_layer_authenticity_audit.py 12 lines: the GUARD_TARGETS entry for
                                         mutation_marker_lint.py

The tests asserting them survived, so the suite was RED at HEAD -- 3 failed, 24
passed -- and `--check-targets` reported mutation_marker_lint.py as an
UNCLASSIFIED GUARD. The commit message of 5e74c37b7c claimed the lint was in
GUARD_TARGETS with the reason written down. That claim was false at HEAD, through
no fault of its author.

After restore: 27 passed, and --check-targets exits 0 with every wired guard
classified.

WHAT THIS INCIDENT IS. The checkpoint path stages everything and skips hooks. It
deleted the deliverable built that same hour to stop findings being silently lost,
and no hook fired, because that path does not run them. Found by an independent
adversarial reviewer looking at something else -- not by a guard, not by CI, and
not by me. Nothing in the repository would have reported it.

Restored from git rather than re-derived, per the never-prune rule: the work
existed and was correct, so the record should show it removed and put back, not
rewritten.

